        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "So what I have at work is many shennanigans to workaround. As example, I need to use the shell to workaround different programs writing to stderr and stdout. However, unfortunately, for example Python buffers outputs unless in shell mode, so stuff to stdout comes after stderr or vice versa if I write it to a file.\r\n\r\nUsually, I would use\r\n```lua\r\n-- repeat last command\r\n-- r means repeat last command\r\nmap('n', ';rj', [[<cmd>lua require(\"harpoon.term\").sendCommand(1, \"!!\\n\")<CR>]], opts) -- r for repeat\r\n```\r\nafter a bug fix to get the output and then ideally `[p` + `/<FAIL\\>` to search from last prompt to see if a test failure occured, because the default output formatting of Python is unusable for parsing.\r\n\r\nThe prompt looks roughly like this and any other kind of line pattern jumping as lib sounds also useful to me:\r\n```txt\r\nlastname@domain.local@GROUP111 ~/directory (master)> ./repl.py -arg1 -tc testname_2.py\r\npid: blablabla\r\narg: -args1\r\narg: -tc\r\narg. testname_2.py\r\nnew file:   /tpm/directory/process1.log\r\nnew file:   /tpm/directory/process2.log\r\n\r\n-------------------------------------------------------------\r\n2023-09-12 14:21:31: ---- START ./setup.py ----\r\n\r\nreturncode:  0\r\nname:  ./setup.py\r\nresult:  pass\r\n2023-09-12 14:21:38: ---- END ./setup.py ----\r\n\r\n-------------------------------------------------------------\r\n2023-09-12 14:21:31: ---- START ./testname_2.py ----\r\n\r\n..\r\nsometimeline\r\n2023-09-12 12:21:31.195306 Ipcmessage: data 2 1.000000\r\n..\r\n\r\nreturncode:  0\r\nname:  ./testname_2.py\r\nresult:  pass\r\n2023-09-12 14:21:38: ---- END ./testname_2.py ----\r\nlastname@domain.local@GROUP111 ~/directory (master)> \r\n```\r\n\r\nOf course, ideally one would not need these hacks and ideally one would have languages with dense and machine+human readable file locations + stack traces usable by vim/neovim etc, which could additionally point into the shell history etc.\r\nAnd yes, I know that the shell history is truncated and has no file backup for output etc, which is a shell antifeature.",
