            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "I feel a bit dumb here as I still don't understand what `[p` should do. My best guess is that when it is inside terminal buffer, it should jump at the line describing start of previous shell output? If yes, then it is most certainly won't be a part of 'mini.bracketed' because it varies too much, i.e. doesn't really have more or less universally fixed rule to define \"start of shell output\".\r\n\r\n'mini.bracketed' has general [`advance()`](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/f158f1905286e023d5848ab0b335a1cf5b8c692a/doc/mini-bracketed.txt#L605) which is used in all targets. As an example for such behavior, take a look at [`conflict()` target](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/f158f1905286e023d5848ab0b335a1cf5b8c692a/lua/mini/bracketed.lua#L362).",
